{
  "image_name": "1a3e8e4b7e240f30c49044b9b137f3a6.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes the ATR–CHK1 checkpoint pathway that responds to replication-associated DNA damage and coordinates cell-cycle control. Replication stress or a double-strand break generates single-stranded DNA coated by RPA, which recruits and activates ATR together with ATRIP and TOPBP1. ATR then activates CHK1, shown as the central signaling hub.\n\nFrom CHK1, the diagram emphasizes two broad outputs. First, it promotes genome maintenance during S phase by supporting DNA repair, replication-fork stability, origin firing control, and dNTP supply; these functions are grouped in an inset with examples such as NER/HRR, fork remodeling factors, MCM/FANCI, and RRM2. Second, CHK1 restrains cell-cycle progression through regulation of CDK activators and inhibitors.\n\nThe lower half shows how CHK1 suppresses CDC25 phosphatases and influences WEE1, MYT1, PP2A, and FAM122A to keep CDK1 and CDK2 activity in check. CDK2 is linked to S phase, while CDK1 controls transition through G2 into mitosis, so the overall effect is to delay cell-cycle progression when DNA replication is perturbed. Visually, the network presents CHK1 as integrating damage sensing with replication protection and inhibition of premature mitotic entry.",
